# Tracer: checked predicate premises expose definition consequences to later
# well-definedness obligations in the same quantified scope.
#
# Before (incorrectly rejected):
# prop nonzero_on(E power_set(R), g fn(x E) R):
#     forall x E:
#         g(x) != 0
#
# forall E power_set(R), f, g fn(x E) R:
#     $nonzero_on(E, g)
#     =>:
#         fn(x E) R {f(x) / g(x)} $in fn(x E) R
# Former behavior: well-definedness stored `$nonzero_on(E, g)` without
# unfolding its definition, so it could not prove that `g(x) != 0`.
#
# Now (verified):
prop nonzero_on(E power_set(R), g fn(x E) R):
    forall x E:
        g(x) != 0

forall E power_set(R), f, g fn(x E) R:
    $nonzero_on(E, g)
    =>:
        fn(x E) R {f(x) / g(x)} $in fn(x E) R
# Current behavior: quantified premises are checked in source order, and each
# checked premise plus its sound definition consequences is available while
# checking later premises and conclusions in the same temporary scope.
#
# Boundary: omitting `$nonzero_on(E, g)` still makes the division ill-defined.
# Executable positive and negative regressions live in
